LEAD = {
    "headline": "PURCHASE INTENT NEEDS A MEDIAN 47 SECONDS OF ATTENTION ADDED UP ACROSS EVERY AD A PERSON SEES, AND A QUARTER OF THE HIGHEST-ATTENTION CAMPAIGNS MOVED IT NOT AT ALL",
    "deck": "Lumen Research published this yesterday with Amazon Ads and Cint across 270 cross-media campaigns.  The headline number is a threshold, which is new.  The two numbers underneath it are the ones that change how you cut and schedule a piece of video, and neither of them appears on Lumen's own page.",
    "stamps": [
        ("PPC LAND · 13 OCT", "https://ppc.land/purchase-intent-needs-a-median-47-seconds-of-ad-attention-lumen-finds/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Lumen Research sells attention measurement.  Yesterday it published a study run with Amazon Ads and Cint across <mark>270 cross-media campaigns</mark>, and the sentence everybody will quote is this one: \"The study found a median of 47 seconds of Cumulative Attention is needed for purchase intent\".  Cumulative attention means the total number of seconds a person actually spent looking at your advertising, added up across every impression and every medium, rather than the number of times an ad was served.  Lumen's framing: \"Attention builds up across impressions, and each medium contributes a different amount\".",
        "That is a different idea from frequency.  Frequency counts deliveries.  This counts looking.  Peter Buckley, connection planning director at Meta, summarised the work on LinkedIn and put it more bluntly than Lumen did: \"Cumulative attention is just frequency counted properly: every exposure, weighted by whether anyone looked\".  He also wrote \"Forget frequency\", which is a strong thing to say about the number almost every media plan is still capped on.",
        "Now the figure that lands in an edit suite rather than a media plan.  Buckley reports that <mark>multiple short views drove on average 21 percent more sales lift than one long continuous view</mark>.  He also reports that streaming television followed by online ads produced 30 percent more brand lift than the same two in the opposite order.  If both hold, then the thing you are buying is a seconds budget per person, assembled out of many small pieces in a deliberate order, and a single long watch is the expensive way to fill it.",
        "Then the contradiction, which is the most useful part of the whole thing.  Buckley: \"Around a quarter of the highest-attention campaigns showed no lift in purchase intent\".  And: \"at least a quarter of the lowest-attention ones beat the typical high-attention campaign on awareness\".  So attention seconds are necessary and nowhere near sufficient.  Buying 47 seconds of looking does not buy you an outcome, and a campaign can clear the threshold comfortably and still move nothing.",
        "Here is what you cannot check, and it matters.  The white paper is available only through Amazon representatives, so the method is not public.  Lumen publishes a threshold for purchase intent and not for the other four things it measured.  Nobody states whether the 47 seconds is per person, per campaign or an aggregate.  Nobody states whether the attention was observed with eye tracking or modelled.  There is no distribution published around the median, so the spread is unknown.  Markets, date range and media mix are all undisclosed, and every campaign in the set appears to have run with Amazon as a media partner.  Lumen's own page says 270 campaigns in the body and \"270+\" in its page description.",
        "One more thing worth repeating because it has not been answered.  Rares Rusu, chief executive of 40Folds, asked in the comments whether creative quality is what is actually driving the results.  Nothing in the available material addresses it.  Which is to say the study may have measured how good the ads were and called it attention.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("47 sec", "median cumulative attention before purchase intent moves"),
        ("+21%", "more sales lift from several short views than one long one"),
        ("1 in 4", "highest-attention campaigns with no purchase-intent lift at all"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Lumen Research sells attention measurement, Amazon Ads sells the inventory the study flatters, and Cint supplied the respondent-level brand lift data.  The three most quotable figures in this item — the 21 percent, the 30 percent and the quarter of campaigns with no lift — are not on Lumen's page at all.  They come from a LinkedIn summary written by a connection planning director at Meta, which also sells video inventory measured this way.  The full paper is gated behind Amazon representatives, so none of the method can be independently checked, and PPC Land says plainly that it cannot tell whether attention here was observed or predicted.",
    "so_what": "The threshold is the headline, but the mechanism is additive rather than instantaneous: attention accrues across many small exposures, and the order of the media matters to the total.  That is why several short views beat one long one by 21 percent on sales lift in this reading, and it is why a frequency cap set in impressions is capping the wrong unit.  Then the quarter of high-attention campaigns that moved nothing tells you the rest: seconds of looking are the entry fee, not the result.  Treat 47 seconds as a floor you have to clear before the creative is even allowed to be the variable.",
    "do_this": "Take your next video campaign and write down, per person, how many seconds of actual looking the plan is trying to buy and in what order the media deliver them.  Then brief a short-exposures version against your current single long placement and run them against each other, so you are testing the finding rather than quoting it.  And when a seller puts 47 seconds in a deck, ask them one question before anything else: per person, or per campaign?",
}

